--- craft/components.md ---
# Components: reuse, adapt, adopt or build

`examples/components/` is a toolkit at authority level 7 (`principles/authority.md`). It gives leverage; it is not a design system the build must use, and it is never a visual ceiling. Excellent results can come from library components, from external ones, from custom ones, or from a deliberate mix.

## The selection order

For each non-trivial piece of UI (from the build plan, Checkpoint 4):

1. **The project's own system first.** An existing codebase's components, tokens and UI kit are authority level 3. Extend them before adding anything.
2. **Inspect the relevant built-in components** (`node scripts/search.mjs components "<effect or pattern>"`). Read the header: `use-when`, `avoid-when`, `tier`, `deps`, `reduced-motion`, `touch`, `a11y`.
3. **Decide fit** against three things: the chosen concept, the product contract (does it support the journey, the density, the states?), and the stack.
4. **Reuse** when it fits as is. **Adapt** (props, timing, markup, or a fork with its own header) when it is close. **Compose** several when the pattern is a combination.
5. **Research alternatives** when nothing fits or the pattern is standard UI with solved accessibility: headless and registry libraries (shadcn/ui, Radix UI, React Aria, Headless UI, Ark UI), specialised libraries (charts, maps, 3D, virtualised tables, date pickers), open-source repositories. These are examples, not mandatory dependencies.
6. **Build a custom component** when existing options are inadequate, or when the piece is the concept itself (the signature, the hook). Give it the library header so it can be reused.

Say in one line which of reuse, adapt, adopt or build you chose for each piece, and why.

**Never** force a library component into a design because Motomation ships it. **Never** rebuild an accessible, maintained solution (dialog, combobox, menu, tabs, date picker, toast) from scratch without a reason; focus management and keyboard models are where hand-built components fail.

## Before adopting external code or a library

| Check | Question |
|---|---|
| Compatibility | Does it support the project's framework and version (React 19, App Router, SSR, Astro islands)? |
| Maintenance | Released recently, issues answered, not deprecated (`craft/stack.md`, the three checks)? |
| API stability | Pre-1.0, or a history of breaking changes? |
| Accessibility | Keyboard model, focus, ARIA, reduced motion: documented and tested? |
| Responsive and touch | Works at 375px and with touch, not just a mouse? |
| Performance | Bundle cost (bundlephobia or the package's own size), runtime cost, does it tree-shake? Within the tier budget (`craft/performance.md`)? |
| Licence | MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD or ISC for commercial use; attribution recorded when required |
| Security | Install scripts, transitive dependencies, a single unknown maintainer? |
| Worth it | Does the benefit justify a dependency, or would 40 lines of platform code do? |

Respect the user's stack and package constraints. Don't install a dependency to reproduce a reference; don't introduce a second component system next to the project's own. Record each adopted dependency with its checks in `DESIGN.md` under `Dependencies`.

## Found code is not trusted code

Whatever is reused, adapted, adopted or built still passes `gates/rules.md`, the tier budget, the micro-states (`thinking/sequence.md`) and the reduced-motion rules. Read it before shipping it.

--- adapters/vanilla.md ---
# Adapter: Plain HTML / CSS / JS

> **Dependencies (`craft/stack.md`, checked 2026-10-16):** `gsap` 3.15.0 and `lenis` 1.3.27 are approved: active, not deprecated, licence free for commercial use (GSAP Standard no-charge licence; Lenis MIT). Re-check npm and the repo (deprecation, last release, licence) at build time.

The most portable route: works in any host, and in chat tools that can only produce one file.

```html
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/gsap@3/dist/gsap.min.js" defer></script>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/gsap@3/dist/ScrollTrigger.min.js" defer></script>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/gsap@3/dist/SplitText.min.js" defer></script>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/lenis@1/dist/lenis.min.js" defer></script>
```

- Pin versions in production (e.g. `gsap@3.15.0`) and self-host if you can.
- Add `class="js"` to `<html>` in an inline script, so CSS can hide static fallbacks only when JS runs.
- Wrap all motion in `gsap.matchMedia()` exactly as in the framework examples.
- A complete single-file reference lives in `examples/html/motomation-film.html`.

--- principles/dials.md ---
# The Three Dials

Every design sets three dials, each at **1, 2 or 3**. There are three levels, not ten, because a model and a reviewer can reliably tell calm from bold, but cannot reliably tell a 6 from a 7. Hold the dials across the site; a product may set them per route family (a store's launch story at MOTION 3, its checkout at MOTION 1), and says so.

| Dial | 1 (Calm) | 2 (Balanced) | 3 (Bold) | What it controls |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **ENERGY** | Still, lots of air | Confident, clear contrast | Loud, huge type, strong colour moments | Type scale jumps, contrast, accent strength |
| **RHYTHM** | Uniform, predictable sections | Consistent with deliberate breaks | Every section a different composition | Layout variation, grid breaks, asymmetry |
| **MOTION** | Hover/focus/press only | Reveals, pinned moments, kinetic type | Scroll-as-film, choreography, WebGL | Motion tier and quantity |

## Dials → motion tier

| MOTION dial | Default tier | Allowed range |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Tier 1 | 1 |
| 2 | Tier 2 | 1–3 |
| 3 | Tier 2–3; Tier 5 for a chapter only when the story has media to scrub | 2–5 (Tier 4 only if the concept is spatial) |

## Where the settings come from

There are no presets. Each dial is set from the brief: what the visitor must do, how the brand should feel in the brief's own words, and what the concept needs. When a visual language helps, `styles/` is the reference: each row of `data/styles.csv` carries the energy, rhythm and motion that language usually runs at. Treat those as a starting point to argue with, not a value to copy.

Set dials directly with `--energy=3 --rhythm=2 --motion=3`.

## Levers (how dials become decisions)

- **A clear first read per screen.** On storytelling screens that is usually one focal point (H-focal); on dense product screens it is a reading order (title → controls → results).
- **Hierarchy by contrast:** size, weight and colour differ on purpose.
- **Whitespace as structure:** space groups and separates; it is not leftover.
- **Disciplined accent:** zero accents is sterile; accent everywhere is noise (H-one-accent). Colour that carries information or brand is not accent.
- **An identity motif:** a gesture, a shape, a typographic habit or a system quality (density, rhythm, precision) that repeats and makes the site belong to its brand.

## Example settings

- Motion designer portfolio: ENERGY 3 · RHYTHM 2 · MOTION 3
- Boutique hotel: ENERGY 1 · RHYTHM 2 · MOTION 2
- Developer tool: ENERGY 2 · RHYTHM 1 · MOTION 2
- Government service: ENERGY 1 · RHYTHM 1 · MOTION 1
- Music festival: ENERGY 3 · RHYTHM 3 · MOTION 3
- B2B procurement catalogue: ENERGY 1 · RHYTHM 1 · MOTION 1 (identity from type and density)
- General marketplace: ENERGY 2 · RHYTHM 1 · MOTION 1–2 (storytelling only on campaign surfaces)

These are illustrations. The brief, the contract and research set the dials; `principles/heuristics.md` → H-rhythm explains when RHYTHM 3 hurts.

--- principles/typography.md ---
# Typography

Typography is the main tool of this skill. Choose type first, before colour or layout, on every brief. Then choose **one of two modes**. They are a fork, not a default with exceptions: each is a complete way of designing, and neither is what you fall back to when the other doesn't fit.

| | HOUSE STYLE (typography-led) | SERVICE STYLE (information-led) |
|---|---|---|
| Default for | Portfolio, studio, editorial, brand, launch and manifesto sites | Dashboards, SaaS product and admin UIs, dense service UIs: booking, checkout, catalogues with filters, data tools |
| What the visitor came for | An impression: who this is and why it is different | A task: read, compare, find, act |
| The hero | The type itself. One word or line carries the screen | The data, the product UI or the imagery. Type makes it legible |
| Scale | Display scale (§3) | Compact scale (§3) |
| Numbers | Can be set as display | Tabular figures (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`), aligned in columns |
| Density | Generous: few elements, large measure of space | Dense but legible: rows, labels, small steps |
| Display face | Chosen for character, at dramatic size | Optional. Often one family at several weights |
| Motion | Can carry the story (Tier 2–5) | Feedback and state changes first (Tier 1–2); a signature lives in one place |

**State the mode in the plan, with the reason from the brief:** `Type mode : SERVICE STYLE (operators scan 40 rows a minute; the numbers are the content)`. A brief that sits between the two (a SaaS marketing site, a hotel, an e-commerce product page) still picks one and says why. A SaaS marketing site that sells through a product demo is usually SERVICE STYLE in the product frame and may use a HOUSE STYLE headline above it; say which part is which.

Both modes keep the same standards: size floors, contrast, measure, a reasoned choice of face (Law 2 in `SKILL.md`; standards vs defaults in `principles/restraint.md`). SERVICE STYLE is not "house style with smaller type", and HOUSE STYLE is not a licence to drop legibility.

## 1. Choose with a reason

Write one line: *"<Display> because <reason tied to the concept>; <Text> because it stays out of the way."*

- Concept about precision or engineering: a grotesk with tight apertures or a mono.
- Concept about heritage, craft or slowness: a serif with visible stroke contrast or calligraphic roots.
- Concept about film, sport or speed: a condensed display with a width axis.
- Concept about play: a face with unusual shapes, used **only** at display sizes.

Search the curated list: `node scripts/search.mjs fonts "<words from the brief>"`. `data/fonts.csv` lists source, license, axes and good pairings.

## 2. Banned as display faces

<!--rule:banned_display_fonts-->Inter, Roboto, Open Sans, Lato, Poppins, Montserrat, Arial, Helvetica or the system UI stack<!--/rule-->. They're fine as **text** faces when chosen deliberately, never as the identity. Also avoid using <!--rule:caution_fonts-->Playfair Display and Space Grotesk<!--/rule--> on autopilot: they became AI defaults. The lists live in `data/rules.csv`.

## 3. Scale

**Display scale (HOUSE STYLE).** Use a fluid, dramatic scale. A good starting point:

```css
:root {
  --step--1: clamp(0.83rem, 0.80rem + 0.15vw, 0.94rem);  /* captions, mono labels */
  --step-0:  clamp(1.00rem, 0.95rem + 0.25vw, 1.19rem);  /* body */
  --step-1:  clamp(1.25rem, 1.10rem + 0.70vw, 1.75rem);  /* lead */
  --step-2:  clamp(1.75rem, 1.40rem + 1.80vw, 3.00rem);  /* section titles */
  --step-3:  clamp(2.75rem, 1.80rem + 4.80vw, 6.50rem);  /* page titles */
  --step-4:  clamp(4.00rem, 1.50rem + 12vw, 14rem);      /* hook / display */
}
```

**Compact scale (SERVICE STYLE).** Small steps, nothing in `vw` above the page title, numbers in tabular figures:

```css
:root {
  --ui--1: 0.75rem;   /* table meta, axis labels (12px floor) */
  --ui-0:  0.875rem;  /* dense table rows, controls */
  --ui-1:  1rem;      /* body, form fields */
  --ui-2:  1.25rem;   /* panel titles */
  --ui-3:  clamp(1.5rem, 1.3rem + 0.8vw, 2rem);  /* page title: the largest type on the screen */
  --ui-kpi: clamp(1.75rem, 1.4rem + 1.2vw, 2.5rem); /* key figures, tabular-nums */
}
```

The page's character comes from the face, the weights, the numerals and the rhythm of rows, not from size.

- Display sizes in `vw` (via `clamp`) so the word fills the composition at every width (HOUSE STYLE only).

### Long words at 375 (check by reading, no browser)

For every display heading, take its **longest word** and the font size the `clamp()` resolves to at 375px (the minimum, or `preferred` worked out at 3.75px per `vw`). Estimate the word's width:

`width ≈ characters × font-size × k`. Use k ≈ 0.45 for condensed faces, 0.5 for serifs, 0.55 for grotesks, 0.65 for wide or extended faces, and +0.1 for all caps.

The word must fit in the 375px content box (≈ 335px with 20px gutters). If it doesn't, lower the `clamp()` minimum to `335 / (characters × k)`, or change the line. Don't fix it with `overflow-wrap: anywhere`, which breaks the word mid-letter. Example: "Transparency" is 12 characters in a grotesk, 335 / (12 × 0.55) ≈ 50px, so a `clamp(4rem, …)` (64px) minimum overflows.

- **Never build one word from separate moving parts.** A word split across door leaves, panels or columns that move apart reads as two words ("Have li"). Split at a word boundary, or keep the word in one element and move it as one piece.
- **Small text sizes are floors.** Body ≥ 16px; captions and mono labels ≥ 12px; under 14px over an image only on a solid chip (see `craft/accessibility.md`).
- Body text never below 16px. Measure 60–75 characters (`max-width: 68ch`).
- Line-height: display 0.85–1.0, headings 1.05–1.2, body 1.5–1.65.

## 4. Details that separate designed from generated

- `text-wrap: balance` on headings and `text-wrap: pretty` on paragraphs.
- Optical tracking: tighten large type (-0.02em to -0.05em); open small caps and mono (+0.06em to +0.12em).
- Real typographic characters: curly quotes (" " ' '), en dash for ranges (10–20), em dash or spaced en dash for breaks, true ellipsis (…), non-breaking spaces before units.
- `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` for prices, tables, counters and timecodes.
- Hanging punctuation for pull quotes (`hanging-punctuation: first` where supported; otherwise a negative indent).
- One typographic motif repeated through the site: index numbers `(01)`, folio marks, a dot leader, a superscript counter.

## 5. Variable fonts as motion

Variable axes are your cheapest high-impact motion:
- `wght` (weight) reacting to cursor proximity, see `signatures/type-signatures.md`.
- `wdth` (width) stretching with scroll velocity.
- `opsz` (optical size) adjusting automatically with `font-optical-sizing: auto`.
- Custom axes (e.g. `SOFT`, `WONK` in Fraunces; `YEAR` in Climate Crisis) as concept carriers.

Animate `font-variation-settings` with GSAP or CSS. Keep the change **local** (letters near the cursor), not whole paragraphs, to avoid reflow-heavy work.

## 6. Kinetic type rules

1. **Split with care.** Use GSAP SplitText (`type: "lines,words"`, `mask: "lines"`, `autoSplit: true`). It keeps accessibility labels and re-splits on resize. Never split into characters for body text.
2. **Reveal by lines** with a mask, staggered 0.06–0.12s. Characters only for short display words.
3. **Readable at every frame.** If a frame mid-animation is unreadable for longer than 300ms, it's too much.
4. **Reduced motion:** text appears instantly in its final state.

## 7. Loading

- Self-host or use `next/font`. Subset to the languages you need.
- Preload only the display face used above the fold.
- `font-display: swap`, with metric-compatible fallbacks (`size-adjust`, `ascent-override`) to avoid layout shift.
- Two families at most, three including a mono.

## 8. Pairing method

1. Pick the display face from the concept.
2. Choose a text face that **contrasts in one dimension only**: serif vs sans, or wide vs narrow, never both.
3. Check both at 16px and at 160px side by side.
4. Check language support (Bangla, Arabic, CJK) if the audience needs it. For Bangla, pair with Hind Siliguri, Noto Sans Bengali or Noto Serif Bengali, and test conjuncts.

--- principles/color.md ---
# Colour

Colour in this house style is quiet by default and loud at one moment.

## Method

1. **Ground:** choose the background temperature from the concept (warm paper, cool concrete, dark room).
2. **Ink:** the text colour, never pure black on pure white unless starkness is the concept. Aim for 12:1 or higher contrast for body text on the ground.
3. **Muted:** a secondary text and hairline colour, at 4.5:1 or higher for any text.
4. **Accent:** one hue, chosen from the concept (safelight red for a darkroom, brass for a hotel, signal green for a terminal).
5. **Surface:** at most one extra tone for cards or panels, 3–6% away from the ground.

Search curated palettes with `node scripts/search.mjs palettes "<words from the brief>"` and verify every pair with `node scripts/contrast.mjs <fg> <bg>`.

## Tokens

```css
:root {
  --ground: #F3F0EA;
  --surface: #EAE6DE;
  --ink: #151412;
  --muted: #5E5A53;
  --line: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink) 14%, transparent);
  --accent: #D9481E;
  --accent-ink: #FFFFFF;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { /* only if the concept supports both */ }
```

Use OKLCH or `color-mix(in oklab, …)` for tints so they stay perceptually even.

## Rules

- **Accent footprint ≤ 5% of any screen.** Reserve it for the action, the active state or the signature.
- **No default gradients.** If you use one, it must come from the concept (a sunset hotel, a heat map) and be built in OKLCH to avoid muddy middles.
- **Dark mode is a design, not an inversion.** Lower the contrast of large surfaces slightly, raise the accent's lightness, and swap shadows for hairlines.
- **Images set the palette.** When photography leads, sample the ground and accent from the photos.
- **Contrast is non-negotiable:** 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for large text (24px+, or 18.66px+ bold) and UI boundaries.

--- principles/layout.md ---
# Layout and Space

## Grid

- 12 columns on desktop, 6 on tablet, 4 on mobile. Outer margin `clamp(20px, 5vw, 96px)`, gutter `clamp(12px, 1.6vw, 24px)`.
- Make the grid **visible in the logic, not in lines**: align captions, numbers and edges so the eye feels the structure.
- Break the grid **once per screen**, on purpose: a word that bleeds off the edge, an image that crosses the gutter, a number hanging in the margin.

## Composition patterns that avoid the template

- **Asymmetric split:** content on columns 1–7, a single detail on 10–12, air between them.
- **Margin notes:** captions and index numbers in a narrow side column, as in a book.
- **Edge anchoring:** headline pinned to the bottom-left of the viewport, details at the top-right.
- **Full-bleed punctuation:** one full-width image or word between dense sections.
- **Stacked index:** a huge list where each row is a link; previews appear on hover.
- **Overlap with purpose:** type over image only where contrast is guaranteed (a scrim, or a solid area of the photo).

## Spacing scale

8px base. Section spacing in large steps: `96px / 160px / 240px` desktop, roughly 60% of that on mobile. Inside components: `4 / 8 / 12 / 16 / 24 / 32`.

## Hero rules

- The hero states the concept, not the company description.
- Don't default to: a centred headline, a subtitle and two buttons. Choose from: edge-anchored type, type-as-image, a pinned stage, an index, a letter, a single object.
- The first screen must hold **one** focal point.

## Navigation and footer

Vary them as part of the structure (see `structures/archetypes.md`). The footer is the sign-off: a giant wordmark, a colophon, a last signature moment. Not a link dump.

## Containers and cards

- Prefer open layouts over boxes. Use a card only when the content is a discrete object (a product, a room, a post).
- No shadow + border + radius + gradient all at once. Pick one way to separate.
- Radius scale with intent: 0 for editorial or brutalist, 2–6px for precise UIs, 16px+ only for soft or playful brands.

--- craft/copy.md ---
# Copy and Microcopy

Generic copy is the fastest way to make a beautiful page feel AI-made. Words are design material.

Loaded on every build and redesign (SKILL.md). The twenty rules below come first; the older sections after them
(banned phrases, headlines, the copy pass, microcopy) go into more detail on the same ground. Rules marked **C** are
measured by `scripts/gate.mjs` (axis C); the rest are checked by reading, in `gates/delivery-gate.md` "Copy".

## The twenty rules

| ID | Rule (what) | Why | Good | Bad | Check |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| copy-01 | Headlines are specific | A generic line fits any company, so it sticks to none of them | "Deploys in 90 seconds, on a third of the servers" | "Take your business to the next level" | read |
| copy-02 | Write for someone in a hurry | People skim. Most words on a page are never read | Short sentences that start with a verb: "Book a room. Pay at the desk." | A sentence with three clauses and two asides before its point | read |
| copy-03 | Sell above the fold | Many visitors never scroll, so the first screen carries the offer | The first screen says what it is, who it's for and the next step | The offer first appears three screens down, after a brand film | read |
| copy-04 | No em dashes in visible copy | They have become a tell of generated text. A full stop or a comma reads cleaner | "Fast. Simple. Reliable." | "Fast — simple — reliable" | **C** |
| copy-05 | Benefit first, then the feature | The feature says what it is; the benefit says why it matters to the reader | "Ship in minutes (one command deploys it)" | "One-command deploy" | read |
| copy-06 | Break up blocks of text | A wall of text looks like work, so it gets skipped | Paragraphs of 2 to 4 lines | A 12-line paragraph on a landing page | **C** (paragraph length, advisory) |
| copy-07 | Cut padding | Every extra word thins out the ones that matter | "We build fast sites." | "We are a company that builds websites that are fast." | read |
| copy-08 | Make it scannable | Eyes jump between headings, bold words and list items | A heading, three bullets, one short paragraph | The whole section in running prose | read |
| copy-09 | No aphorism formulas | "It's not X, it's Y" is a cliché; readers spot the shape and stop reading | State the point: "Pages that load before the tap lands" | "It's not a website, it's an experience" | **C** |
| copy-10 | The body backs the headline | A headline the body doesn't support reads as a bait-and-switch | Headline "Own your data", body explains self-hosting and export | Headline "Own your data", body is about pricing tiers | read |
| copy-11 | No warm-up openers | The first line is the most-read line; spend it on the claim | Start with the claim: "Invoices that chase themselves" | "In today's fast-paced world, businesses need…" | **C** (opener list) |
| copy-12 | Remove what doesn't move the reader | Each sentence costs attention that the next one needs | Only what helps the visitor decide or act | Company history, caveats and tangents on the landing page | read |
| copy-13 | Answer objections before the CTA | Unanswered doubt stops the click at the last step | "No card needed. Cancel in one click." right above the button | Objections answered only on a separate FAQ page | read |
| copy-14 | One to three key points per section | Past three, none of them lands | Three features, one clearly first | Eight bullets with equal weight | read |
| copy-15 | No two-beat antithesis | "Not just X, but Y" is overused and reads as filler | "Design and engineering from one person" | "Not just a designer, but an engineer" | **C** |
| copy-16 | No claims you can't prove | Numbers and logos without a source cost more trust than they win when found out | "1,240 shops on the platform (dashboard, 1 Oct 2026)" | "Trusted by 10,000+ teams", no source | read (fixed list flagged by **C**) |
| copy-17 | CTAs: a clear verb and its outcome | The reader should know what happens after the click | "Start a free trial", "Book a table for tonight" | "Learn more", "Submit", "Click here" | **C** (vague-label list) |
| copy-18 | Talk to one buyer | Copy written for everyone persuades no one in particular | "For founders shipping their first product" | "For businesses of all sizes" | read |
| copy-19 | One idea per section | A section that answers two questions answers neither well | A section that answers "How much does it cost?" | A section that mixes pricing with the team's story | read |
| copy-20 | Proof sits next to the claim | Proof one page away might as well not exist | A customer quote right under the feature it vouches for | All testimonials in one carousel at the bottom | read |

A rule marked **C** fails the gate when it finds a violation in visible text (body copy, headings, buttons, `alt`,
`<title>` and the meta description). Code, `<pre>`, `<code>` and `[NEEDS COPY: …]` markers are skipped.

## Rules
1. **Specific beats impressive.** "Twelve rooms on a 200-year-old lane in Puran Dhaka", not "An oasis of luxury".
2. **Use the brief's facts.** Names, places, materials, dates, real numbers. If a fact is missing, write `[NEEDS COPY: …]`. Never invent it.
3. **Headlines say one thing.** Under 8 words for a hook. No two-part "X. Y." formula on every section.
4. **Write like a person talks.** Contractions, short sentences, active verbs.
5. **Buttons say what happens.** "Book a room", "See the work", "Start free". Not "Learn more" or "Submit".

## Banned phrases (AI tells)
"Unlock", "elevate", "seamless", "revolutionise", "empower", "cutting-edge", "next-level", "in today's fast-paced world", "we're passionate about", "crafting digital experiences", "your journey", "game-changer", "delve", "harness the power", "look no further", "take it to the next level", "transform the way you…".

## Patterns to avoid
- Three adjectives in a row ("fast, simple and powerful").
- Rhetorical questions as headlines ("Ready to grow?").
- "It's not just X, it's Y."
- Every section ending with a CTA.
- Fake specificity ("Trusted by 10,000+ teams" with no source).
- Emoji as bullet points.

## Headlines

A headline is a design element with one job: say the page's idea in words the reader would use.

1. **Name the thing.** A noun the visitor knows ("Twelve rooms", "A terminal for invoices") beats an abstraction ("Hospitality, reimagined").
2. **One claim per headline.** If it needs "and", it's two headlines, or one of them is a subhead.
3. **No inflated stakes.** Cut words that make the subject sound historic or profound ("a testament to", "redefining", "the future of", "more than ever"). If the fact is remarkable, the fact does the work.
4. **Vary the shape across sections.** If three section heads share a pattern (all questions, all "The X of Y", all two-beat fragments), rewrite two of them.
5. **Sentence case**, unless the brand's type system says otherwise. Title Case On Every Word reads as a template.
6. **Read it at 375px.** A headline that breaks into a dangling one-word last line gets rewritten, not just re-sized.

## The copy pass (run before the Delivery Gate)

Read every visible string once, top to bottom, and fix these AI tells. They are patterns of rhythm and filler, not banned words, so they slip past a phrase list.

| Tell | Looks like | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Padding tails | a sentence ending in an "-ing" clause that adds a vague benefit ("…, ensuring a smooth stay") | Delete the tail, or make it a concrete second sentence |
| Borrowed authority | "experts agree", "studies show", "many believe" with no source | Name the source, or cut the claim |
| Hedge stacks | "may potentially help", "can often sometimes" | One verb, stated plainly |
| Dash habit | an em dash anywhere in visible copy | Use a full stop, a comma or brackets. Visible copy has no em dashes (rule copy-04 below, gate axis C) |
| Synonym cycling | the same thing called a "space", then a "venue", then a "destination" | Pick the brief's word and keep it |
| Mirror sentences | "Not just a hotel. A home." on several sections | Keep at most one on the page |
| Generic closers | every section ends with an invitation ("Discover more", "Start your journey") | One clear action per screen, and only where the visitor can act |
| Placeholder-sounding names | "Acme", "Jane Doe", "Lorem" left in labels, alt text or meta tags | Use the brief's names or a `[NEEDS COPY]` marker |

The pass is done when every row is "none found" or fixed. Record leftovers as `[NEEDS COPY: …]` for the user.

## Microcopy
- Empty states explain what to do next.
- Errors say what happened and how to fix it, in plain words, next to the field.
- Loading states say what is loading when it takes longer than 1 second.
- Confirmations are specific: "Room booked for 12–14 March", not "Success!".

--- craft/seo.md ---
# SEO checklist

Twenty items, loaded on every build and redesign (SKILL.md). Each is tagged:

- **[M] mechanical**: a tool can decide it. Items marked **gate S** are measured on every page by `scripts/gate.mjs`
  (axis S, gate/4). The other [M] items are site-level (they need the whole site, a server or a crawl), so they are
  ticked in `gates/delivery-gate.md` "SEO" with the command or file that proves them.
- **[H] human**: the site owner does it in an outside tool. Write it in the hand-off as "owner to do", never as done.

The older notes in `craft/seo-performance.md` (content in the HTML, JSON-LD types per category, `hreflang`) still apply.

| ID | Tag | Item | How it is decided |
|---|---|---|---|
| seo-01 | [M] | `sitemap.xml` exists and lists every public route | site-level: compare the sitemap with the route list |
| seo-02 | [M] | `robots.txt` exists, with no accidental `Disallow: /` | site-level: read the file |
| seo-03 | [M] | No `noindex` on public pages | **gate S** (a `noindex` robots meta on the page) |
| seo-04 | [M] | A canonical tag on every route except error pages | **gate S** (exactly one `<link rel="canonical">` with an absolute URL). **Exempt:** 404, 500 and other error pages: a response with status ≥ 400, or a page named `404.html`, `500.html`, `error.html`, `not-found`, `_error`. An error page has no canonical URL: it is served at whatever address failed, and a canonical there would point every broken URL at one indexable page (a soft 404). A canonical present on an error page is a warning |
| seo-05 | [M] | Meta title unique per page, 60 characters or fewer | **gate S** (present, ≤ 60); uniqueness is site-level |
| seo-06 | [M] | Meta description unique per page, 160 characters or fewer | **gate S** (present, ≤ 160); uniqueness is site-level |
| seo-07 | [M] | Exactly one `<h1>` per page | **gate S** |
| seo-08 | [M] | Heading order skips no levels (no `h2` → `h4`) | **gate S** |
| seo-09 | [M] | Alt text on every meaningful image; decorative images use `alt=""` | gate `a` count (names check) |
| seo-10 | [M] | Schema markup where the page is eligible (Product, Article, FAQ, Event, Organization) | **gate S** warns when no JSON-LD block parses; eligibility is a judgement |
| seo-11 | [M] | At least 3 internal links per page (every other page when `sitemap.xml` lists fewer than 3; a one-page site needs none) | **gate S** (same-origin or relative `href`, not `#` alone) |
| seo-12 | [M] | Zero broken links | site-level: a link checker over the built site |
| seo-13 | [M] | Images compressed: WebP or AVIF, responsive sizes (`srcset`/`sizes` or `<picture>`) | gate axis P (perf-01) |
| seo-14 | [M] | Core Web Vitals targets: LCP ≤ 2.5 s, INP ≤ 200 ms, CLS ≤ 0.1 | field data or Lighthouse; never claimed unmeasured (`craft/performance.md`) |
| seo-15 | [M] | Mobile layout works at 375 px with no overflow | gate `o` and `w` counts |
| seo-16 | [M] | HTTPS enforced; no mixed content | **gate S** (no `http://` subresource in the page); the redirect is site-level |
| seo-17 | [M] | URL slugs: lowercase, hyphens, no IDs, no query parameters | **gate S** (internal `href`s and the canonical) |
| seo-18 | [M] | `og:image` present, 1200 × 630 | **gate S** (`og:image` present; `og:image:width`/`height` = 1200/630 when stated) |
| seo-19 | [H] | Search Console verified and the sitemap submitted | owner |
| seo-20 | [M] | `llms.txt` current: names the version and the latest report | site-level; for this repository, the root check-repo script |

## What gate S counts

Each failing item on the page adds one to the S count in the stamp (`rules: C0 S0 P0 K0`). The page passes S at 0.
Local files have no real origin, so a relative canonical is accepted for `file:` and `localhost` targets and reported
as "relative (local)". Schema markup only warns: whether a page is eligible is a judgement the gate does not make.

## Writing the tags

- Title: the page's subject first, the brand last: "Twelve rooms on Shankhari Bazar · Ghor Hotel".
- Description: one sentence a person would click, with the brief's facts, ≤ 160 characters.
- Canonical: the absolute URL the page should rank under, the same one the sitemap lists.
- `og:image`: a designed 1200 × 630 image, not a cropped screenshot. State `og:image:width` and `og:image:height`.

--- craft/performance.md ---
# Performance

Motion only feels premium when it is smooth. A dropped frame reads as a cheap site, whatever the idea was. Budgets are part of the design, decided before the build, not discovered after it.

## Targets (field data, mid-range phone, 4G)
- **LCP** ≤ 2.5s · **INP** ≤ 200ms · **CLS** ≤ 0.1
- 60fps on desktop for all scroll animations; 30fps or better on mid-range phones for Tier 4–5, otherwise use the fallback.
- One frame is 16.7ms at 60Hz and 8.3ms at 120Hz. The browser needs part of that for style, layout, paint and composite, so script work per frame should stay under about 6ms.

**Never claim scores you didn't measure.** If you can, run Lighthouse or check field data and report the real numbers. Otherwise say "not measured".

## JavaScript budget (gzipped, first load)
| Tier | Budget |
|---|---|
| 1 | ≤ 30KB beyond the framework |
| 2 | ≤ 80KB (gsap core ≈ 25KB, ScrollTrigger ≈ 15KB, SplitText ≈ 7KB, lenis ≈ 4KB) |
| 3 | ≤ 140KB (+ Lottie/Rive runtime, loaded when needed) |
| 4–5 | ≤ 250KB + assets loaded after first paint; Three.js imported dynamically |

## What each property costs

The browser turns a change into pixels in up to three stages. The fewer stages a property touches, the cheaper every frame is.

| Cost | Properties | Use |
|---|---|---|
| Composite only | `transform`, `opacity` | Routine motion: reveals, parallax, hovers, page transitions |
| Composite in current engines, paint in some | `clip-path` (basic shapes: `inset()`, `circle()`, `polygon()`) | When the shape change **is** the concept (an aperture opening, a wipe between chapters). Ship with the checks below |
| Paint every frame | `filter` (blur, drop-shadow), `box-shadow`, `background-position`, `mask`, gradients that move, `backdrop-filter` | Rarely, on small areas, with a measured frame time and a static fallback |
| Layout every frame | `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`, `padding`, `font-size`, `grid-template-*` | Never animated. Use `transform: scale()` / `translate()` and the FLIP technique instead |

## Rules you can check by reading the code

Each rule names what to look for in the source, so a reviewer can pass or fail it without a browser.

**P1. Routine motion uses `transform` and `opacity`.** Look at every `transition`, `@keyframes`, `gsap.to/from`, and every `style.x =` inside a rAF or scroll handler. Anything that changes a layout property (table above) fails.
Why: layout changes force the browser to recompute the position of everything after the element, every frame.

**P2. Paint-heavy effects carry a performance check and a static fallback.** Any animated `clip-path`, `filter`, `mask`, `backdrop-filter` or moving gradient needs, next to it: (a) a size limit (the effect covers one element, not the page, or the reason it must is written down), (b) a reduced-motion path that shows the end state, and (c) a fallback that keeps the meaning when the effect is off (content visible, not clipped to nothing).
Why: paint cost scales with the painted area. A blur on a 300px card is cheap; the same blur on a 2560px hero at DPR 2 is the most common cause of jank on motion sites.

**P3. No layout reads inside a write loop.** Inside a scroll handler, rAF callback or `onUpdate`, there are no calls to `getBoundingClientRect`, `offsetTop/Width/Height`, `scrollHeight` or `getComputedStyle` after a style write in the same frame. Measure once (on mount and on resize), cache, then only write.
Why: a read after a write forces a synchronous layout, and doing it per element per frame multiplies it.

**P4. Scroll work is driven, not polled.** Scroll-linked motion uses ScrollTrigger, `IntersectionObserver`, or a single passive `scroll` listener that only schedules one rAF. Look for `addEventListener("scroll", …)` without `{ passive: true }`, or handlers that do work directly.
Why: scroll events can fire more often than frames; work belongs in the frame.

**P5. Every loop stops when it can't be seen.** Each `requestAnimationFrame` loop, canvas, WebGL context and `<video>` is paused by an `IntersectionObserver` when off-screen and by `visibilitychange` when the tab is hidden. Search for `requestAnimationFrame(`: each one needs both stop paths and a `cancelAnimationFrame` in cleanup.
Why: off-screen work is the most common hidden cost on animated sites. Users never see it but their battery does.

**P6. Canvas resolution is clamped.** `canvas.width = clientWidth * Math.min(1.5, devicePixelRatio)` or lower; coarse pointers (phones) may drop to half. Look for a bare `devicePixelRatio` multiplication.
Why: at DPR 3 a full-screen canvas has 9× the pixels of DPR 1 for an effect nobody can see that sharply.

**P7. `will-change` is temporary and rare.** It appears on at most a few elements, set when an animation starts and removed when it ends. A stylesheet rule like `* { will-change: transform }` or `will-change` on list items fails.
Why: each promoted layer costs GPU memory; too many and the page slows down instead of speeding up.

**P8. Heavy libraries load late.** Three.js, Lottie, Rive and physics are `await import()`ed when the section that needs them is near the viewport, never in the first bundle.
Why: the first paint should not wait for code that runs three screens down.

**P9. Images and fonts never shift the layout.** Every `<img>`/`<video>` has `width` and `height` (or an `aspect-ratio` box); below-the-fold images are `loading="lazy"` with `sizes`; the LCP image is preloaded with `fetchpriority="high"`; AVIF/WebP with width-based `srcset`; hero images ≤ 200KB. Fonts: preload one file at most, `font-display: swap`, subset, with metric-matched fallbacks (`size-adjust`).
Why: CLS is caused almost entirely by media without reserved space and by late font swaps.

**P10. Resize and refresh are batched.** Resize work is debounced or uses `ResizeObserver`; `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` runs once after fonts and images load, not inside every component.
Why: each refresh re-measures every trigger on the page.

## Worked example: reading a Tier 4 component

`examples/components/backgrounds/liquid-field.tsx` draws a full-bleed WebGL shader, the most expensive kind of effect in the library. Reading it against the rules:

| Rule | What the code does | Result |
|---|---|---|
| P5 | `IntersectionObserver` sets `visible`; `visibilitychange` restarts only when visible; the frame loop only re-arms when `!reduce && visible && !document.hidden`; cleanup calls `cancelAnimationFrame`, disconnects both observers | pass |
| P6 | `scale = (fine ? 1 : 0.5) * Math.min(1.5, devicePixelRatio)` | pass (half resolution on touch) |
| P2 | The canvas sits inside a span that already paints a `linear-gradient` of the same three colours, so a failed context, a lost context or reduced motion still shows the material | pass |
| P10 | `ResizeObserver` resizes the drawing buffer; no window resize listener | pass |
| P9 | `position: absolute; inset: 0` inside the parent, so the canvas never changes layout | pass |

A failing version of the same idea, the kind agents write by default:

```js
// ✗ P1, P3, P4, P6
window.addEventListener("scroll", () => {
  hero.style.height = `${100 - scrollY / 10}vh`;            // layout property, every scroll event
  const top = card.getBoundingClientRect().top;             // read after write: forced layout
  card.style.filter = `blur(${Math.max(0, top / 50)}px)`;   // full-card paint, no fallback
});
canvas.width = innerWidth * devicePixelRatio;               // 9× pixels at DPR 3
```

```js
// ✓ same intent
let cardTop = card.getBoundingClientRect().top + scrollY;  // measured once (and on resize)
let queued = false;
addEventListener("scroll", () => { if (!queued) { queued = true; requestAnimationFrame(frame); } }, { passive: true });
function frame() {
  queued = false;
  hero.style.transform = `scale(${1 - Math.min(scrollY, 400) / 4000})`;  // composite only
  card.style.opacity = String(Math.min(1, Math.max(0, 1 - (cardTop - scrollY) / innerHeight))); // fade, not blur
}
canvas.width = canvas.clientWidth * Math.min(1.5, devicePixelRatio);
```

Reading alone catches all four failures. The Delivery Gate measures the result in a browser; these rules are what you check before it runs.

## The twenty-item checklist

Loaded on every build and redesign (SKILL.md), next to `craft/copy.md`, `craft/seo.md` and `craft/security.md`. Tags:
**[FE]** frontend, **[BE]** backend, **[IN]** infrastructure, **[H]** a human records it. Items marked **gate P** are
measured on every page by `scripts/gate.mjs` (axis P, gate/4); the rest are ticked in `gates/delivery-gate.md`
"Performance" with the file, config or measurement that proves them. A static page with no backend writes "n/a (no
backend)" for the [BE] items, never a tick.

| ID | Tag | Item | How it is decided |
|---|---|---|---|
| perf-01 | [FE] | Images compressed; AVIF or WebP, with a fallback where needed | **gate P**: a raster `<img>` (JPEG, PNG, GIF) with no AVIF/WebP `<source>` fails |
| perf-02 | [FE] | Below-the-fold images use `loading="lazy"` | **gate P**: an `<img>` whose top is below the first 1440 × 900 screen without `loading="lazy"` fails |
| perf-03 | [FE] | The LCP image is not lazy-loaded | **gate P**: the largest image in the first screen with `loading="lazy"` fails |
| perf-04 | [FE] | Code-split: no single script over the tier's budget | **gate P**: gzipped first-load JS beyond the framework over the tier budget above fails; no tier known → the Tier 4–5 budget. The framework is what its build lists as runtime (Next.js: `rootMainFiles` and `polyfillFiles` in `.next/build-manifest.json`, read when a project folder is gated through `out/`); elsewhere, and for a URL target, every script counts. `nomodule` scripts never count |
| perf-05 | [FE] | API responses cached (SWR, React Query, or `Cache-Control`) | read the data layer; "n/a (no API)" on a static page |
| perf-06 | [FE] | A CDN fronts static assets | the deploy target (Pages, Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare) or the CDN config |
| perf-07 | [FE] | JS and CSS minified in production | **gate P** warns when a script over 2 KB looks unminified (long comments, short lines); the build config decides |
| perf-08 | [FE] | No extra re-renders (memo only where the profiler showed a cost) | React Profiler, recorded when used; never `memo` everything by default |
| perf-09 | [FE] | Input handlers debounced (search, resize) | read the handlers; `requestAnimationFrame` or a debounce on every resize/scroll/input listener |
| perf-10 | [FE] | Large lists paginated or virtualised | any list over ~100 items |
| perf-11 | [FE] | Unused packages and dead scripts removed | **gate P**: an inline `<script>` with no code (empty or comments only) fails; packages: `npx depcheck` or reading `package.json` |
| perf-12 | [FE] | Non-critical scripts deferred (analytics, chat) | **gate P**: an external classic `<script>` in `<head>` without `defer`, `async`, `type="module"` or `nomodule` fails (a `nomodule` script, such as Next.js polyfills, is skipped by every browser that runs modules) |
| perf-13 | [FE] | Loading skeletons for async content | read the async views; a skeleton sized like the final content (no layout shift) |
| perf-14 | [IN] | A load balancer when traffic justifies one, and the reason written down | hosting notes |
| perf-15 | [BE] | API payloads trimmed to what the client uses | read the handlers or the GraphQL selection |
| perf-16 | [BE] | Database connections pooled | the DB client config |
| perf-17 | [BE] | Costly query results cached (Redis or in-memory) | the cache layer, with its invalidation rule |
| perf-18 | [BE] | N+1 queries fixed | query log on the slowest route |
| perf-19 | [BE] | Server-side caching on expensive routes | response headers or the framework's route cache |
| perf-20 | [H] | A Lighthouse run recorded with its real numbers | the report file, or "not measured"; never a number from memory |

Each failing gate P item adds one to the P count in the stamp (`rules: C0 S0 P0 K0`); the page passes P at 0.

--- craft/security.md ---
# Security checklist

A beautiful page that leaks a key or trusts the client is not delivered. Loaded on every build and redesign
(SKILL.md); the [M] limits are in `gates/rules.md` (Security), read before design. Tags: **[M] mechanical** (a tool
can decide it) and **[H] human** (the owner, or a scanner the owner runs). Items marked **gate K** are measured on
every page by `scripts/gate.mjs` (axis K, gate/4): failures count in K, heuristic checks print as warnings and are
never counted. The other items are ticked in `gates/delivery-gate.md` "Security" with the file or command that proves
them. A static page with no backend writes "n/a (no backend)" for server items, never a tick.

| ID | Tag | Item | How it is decided |
|---|---|---|---|
| sec-01 | [M] | Protect admin routes: auth required, `noindex` set | route list + middleware; a request without a session gets 401 or a redirect |
| sec-02 | [H] | Server-side permissions: every access check runs on the server | read each protected handler; hiding a button is not a check |
| sec-03 | [M] | Row-level security where applicable | the policy list (`pg_policies`, the Supabase dashboard); every user-data table has RLS on |
| sec-04 | [H] | Verify email addresses on signup | the auth provider's setting or the signup handler |
| sec-05 | [H] | Hash passwords with bcrypt or argon2 | the auth code or the provider's documentation |
| sec-06 | [M] | No auth tokens in local storage: cookies only, `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` | **gate K** checks every `Set-Cookie` on the page response (fails); `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` writes of token-named keys warn |
| sec-07 | [M] | No secrets in client bundles | **gate K** scans the HTML, inline scripts and same-origin scripts for secret-shaped strings (fails) |
| sec-08 | [M] | `.env` files not in git (verify `.gitignore`) | `git check-ignore .env`; **gate K** warns when a local target's project does not ignore `.env` |
| sec-09 | [M] | No sensitive data in logs | grep the log output for the gate K secret patterns, emails and `password`; **gate K** warns on `console.*` calls that print them |
| sec-10 | [H] | All SQL is parameterized | read the data layer; no string-built SQL |
| sec-11 | [M] | Validate all form inputs server-side | every mutation handler parses its input before use |
| sec-12 | [M] | Escape output; no raw `innerHTML` with user input | **gate K** fails an inline script that writes `location`, `search`, `hash` or URL parameters into `innerHTML`/`outerHTML`/`document.write`; the rest by reading |
| sec-13 | [M] | Validate file uploads by MIME and size | the upload handler: allow-list, size limit, storage path |
| sec-14 | [H] | Verify webhook signatures | each webhook handler |
| sec-15 | [M] | Rate-limit requests (auth, contact, mutations) | the middleware or edge config |
| sec-16 | [M] | Tighten CORS; no wildcard in production | **gate K** fails `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the page |
| sec-17 | [M] | Disable production debugging | **gate K** warns on development builds (React development build, source maps on a production URL) |
| sec-18 | [H] | Update vulnerable dependencies | the audit output |
| sec-19 | [H] | Run the security scan; record results | the scan report |
| sec-20 | [M] | Never trust client-supplied values for authorisation | read the handlers: role, user id and price come from the session or the database |

## The twenty rules

Each rule: what it asks, why, one good example, one bad example.

### sec-01 [M] Protect admin routes: auth required, `noindex` set

- **What:** Every admin, dashboard or internal route requires an authenticated session and sends `noindex`.
- **Why:** An unauthenticated admin URL is the shortest path to the data; an indexed one gets found by search engines and scanners.
- **Good:** `middleware.ts` redirects `/admin/*` without a session to `/login`; the admin layout sets `<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">` and `X-Robots-Tag: noindex`.
- **Bad:** `/admin` renders for anyone who knows the URL and is listed in `sitemap.xml`.
- **Decided by:** route list + middleware; a request without a session gets 401 or a redirect

### sec-02 [H] Server-side permissions: every access check runs on the server

- **What:** Every read and write of protected data checks the user's permission in the server handler, never only in the client.
- **Why:** Client code is the visitor's code. Hiding a button stops nobody who can open DevTools or send a request with curl.
- **Good:** `DELETE /api/posts/:id` loads the post and returns 403 unless `post.ownerId === session.userId`.
- **Bad:** The delete button renders only for owners; the endpoint deletes whatever id it receives.
- **Decided by:** read each protected handler; hiding a button is not a check

### sec-03 [M] Row-level security where applicable

- **What:** Where the database supports it (Postgres, Supabase), row-level security is on for every table holding user data, with a policy per operation.
- **Why:** A single missing `WHERE user_id = …` leaks every row; RLS makes the database refuse it even when the code forgets.
- **Good:** `alter table notes enable row level security; create policy own_notes on notes using (user_id = auth.uid());`
- **Bad:** A Supabase table read from the browser with the anon key and RLS disabled.
- **Decided by:** the policy list (`pg_policies`, the Supabase dashboard); every user-data table has RLS on

### sec-04 [H] Verify email addresses on signup

- **What:** A new account cannot act as its email address (sign in to paid features, receive resets, send invites) until the address is confirmed.
- **Why:** Unverified addresses enable account squatting, spam relays and password-reset hijacks of someone else's address.
- **Good:** The auth provider sends a signed, expiring link; `email_verified` is checked before the account is active.
- **Bad:** Signup logs the user straight in and trusts the typed address for resets.
- **Decided by:** the auth provider's setting or the signup handler

### sec-05 [H] Hash passwords with bcrypt or argon2

- **What:** Passwords are stored only as a slow, salted hash: argon2id or bcrypt (cost ≥ 12). Never plain, never a fast hash.
- **Why:** Databases leak. A slow salted hash turns a leak into years of cracking; MD5 or SHA-256 turns it into minutes.
- **Good:** `await argon2.hash(password)` on signup; `argon2.verify(hash, input)` on login.
- **Bad:** `sha256(password)` or the password itself stored in a `password` column.
- **Decided by:** the auth code or the provider's documentation

### sec-06 [M] No auth tokens in local storage: cookies only, `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite`

- **What:** Session and auth tokens live in cookies flagged `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`; never in `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`.
- **Why:** Any script on the page can read web storage, so one XSS hands over the session. An `HttpOnly` cookie can't be read by script.
- **Good:** `Set-Cookie: sid=…; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax`
- **Bad:** `localStorage.setItem('authToken', res.token)`
- **Decided by:** **gate K** checks every `Set-Cookie` on the page response (fails); `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` writes of token-named keys warn

### sec-07 [M] No secrets in client bundles

- **What:** No private key, secret API key, token or password appears in served HTML or JavaScript. Only publishable keys reach the client.
- **Why:** Anything shipped to the browser is public: bundles are cached, mirrored and searched for keys within minutes.
- **Good:** The server calls Stripe with `process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`; the client gets only `pk_live_…`.
- **Bad:** `const stripe = Stripe('sk_live_…')` in a client component, or `NEXT_PUBLIC_OPENAI_KEY`.
- **Decided by:** **gate K** scans the HTML, inline scripts and same-origin scripts for secret-shaped strings (fails)

### sec-08 [M] `.env` files not in git (verify `.gitignore`)

- **What:** `.env` and `.env.*` are listed in `.gitignore`; only `.env.example` with placeholders is committed.
- **Why:** A committed secret stays in history after it's deleted; public repos are scanned for keys continuously.
- **Good:** `.gitignore` has `.env`, `.env.*`, `!.env.example`; `git check-ignore .env` prints `.env`.
- **Bad:** `.env.production` committed "just for the deploy".
- **Decided by:** `git check-ignore .env`; **gate K** warns when a local target's project does not ignore `.env`

### sec-09 [M] No sensitive data in logs

- **What:** Logs and console output never contain tokens, keys, passwords, session ids or email addresses.
- **Why:** Logs travel further than the database: log services, CI output, support screenshots, browser consoles.
- **Good:** `logger.info('login ok', { userId })`
- **Bad:** `console.log('login', user.email, token)`
- **Decided by:** grep the log output for the gate K secret patterns, emails and `password`; **gate K** warns on `console.*` calls that print them

### sec-10 [H] All SQL is parameterized

- **What:** Every query passes values as parameters or through a query builder or ORM; no SQL is built by string concatenation.
- **Why:** String-built SQL is SQL injection: one quote in an input reads or deletes the database.
- **Good:** `db.query('select * from users where email = $1', [email])`
- **Bad:** `` db.query(`select * from users where email = '${email}'`) ``
- **Decided by:** read the data layer; no string-built SQL

### sec-11 [M] Validate all form inputs server-side

- **What:** Every mutation handler parses and validates its input with a schema (Zod or equivalent) before using it; client validation is only for feedback.
- **Why:** Client validation is skipped by anyone who sends the request directly. Unvalidated input reaches the database, the mailer and the logs.
- **Good:** `const data = ContactSchema.parse(await req.json())` with length limits and an email format.
- **Bad:** `await db.insert(messages).values(await req.json())`
- **Decided by:** every mutation handler parses its input before use

### sec-12 [M] Escape output; no raw `innerHTML` with user input

- **What:** User- or URL-supplied text is rendered as text (`textContent`, framework escaping). Raw HTML from users is sanitized (DOMPurify) first.
- **Why:** Writing user input as HTML is cross-site scripting: the attacker's script runs as the visitor, with their session.
- **Good:** `el.textContent = params.get('q')`; React `{q}`.
- **Bad:** `el.innerHTML = location.hash.slice(1)`; `dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: comment }}` unsanitized.
- **Decided by:** **gate K** fails an inline script that writes `location`, `search`, `hash` or URL parameters into `innerHTML`/`outerHTML`/`document.write`; the rest by reading

### sec-13 [M] Validate file uploads by MIME and size

- **What:** The server checks each upload's real type (magic bytes, not the extension) against an allow-list and enforces a size limit; files are stored outside the web root with generated names.
- **Why:** An unchecked upload is a way to host malware, run a script on your domain, or fill the disk.
- **Good:** Accept `image/jpeg`, `image/png`, `image/webp` up to 5 MB, sniffed with `file-type`; saved as `<uuid>.webp`.
- **Bad:** Any file saved to `/public/uploads/` under the name the user gave it.
- **Decided by:** the upload handler: allow-list, size limit, storage path

### sec-14 [H] Verify webhook signatures

- **What:** Each webhook handler verifies the provider's signature (and timestamp) over the raw body before acting.
- **Why:** A webhook URL is public. Without the signature check, anyone can post a fake "payment succeeded".
- **Good:** `stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(rawBody, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET)`
- **Bad:** `if (body.type === 'checkout.session.completed') fulfil(body)` with no signature check.
- **Decided by:** each webhook handler

### sec-15 [M] Rate-limit requests (auth, contact, mutations)

- **What:** Login, signup, password reset, contact forms and every mutation have a per-IP and per-account rate limit.
- **Why:** Without limits, passwords are brute-forced, inboxes flooded and paid APIs drained.
- **Good:** Login: 5 attempts per 15 minutes per account and IP, then 429 with `Retry-After`.
- **Bad:** A contact form that sends an email for every POST, unlimited.
- **Decided by:** the middleware or edge config

### sec-16 [M] Tighten CORS; no wildcard in production

- **What:** CORS allows only the origins that need it, listed by name; credentials are never combined with a wildcard.
- **Why:** `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` lets any site read your API's responses from a visitor's browser.
- **Good:** `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com`
- **Bad:** `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on an authenticated API in production.
- **Decided by:** **gate K** fails `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the page

### sec-17 [M] Disable production debugging

- **What:** Production runs a production build: no debug mode, no verbose error pages, no public source maps, no development React.
- **Why:** Debug output and stack traces map the system for an attacker; source maps publish the source.
- **Good:** `NODE_ENV=production`, `debug: false`, errors return an id; source maps uploaded privately to the error tracker.
- **Bad:** A stack trace with file paths and SQL on the 500 page; `react-dom.development.js` on the live site.
- **Decided by:** **gate K** warns on development builds (React development build, source maps on a production URL)

### sec-18 [H] Update vulnerable dependencies

- **What:** Known-vulnerable dependencies are updated, or each remaining finding is written down with the reason it is safe.
- **Why:** Most breaches use published vulnerabilities in old packages, not new ones.
- **Good:** `npm audit --omit=dev` clean; Dependabot or Renovate on; exceptions listed in `SECURITY.md`.
- **Bad:** A lockfile untouched for two years with critical advisories open.
- **Decided by:** the audit output

### sec-19 [H] Run the security scan; record results

- **What:** Before delivery, a security scan runs (`npm audit`, a secret scanner such as gitleaks, an OWASP ZAP baseline on the URL) and its results are recorded.
- **Why:** An unrecorded scan didn't happen. A recorded one lets the next person see what was found and what was accepted.
- **Good:** `SECURITY.md` lists the scan date, tools, findings and the fix or acceptance for each.
- **Bad:** "Looks secure" in the hand-off with no scan output.
- **Decided by:** the scan report

### sec-20 [M] Never trust client-supplied values for authorisation

- **What:** Role, user id, tenant, price and permissions come from the session or the database, never from the request body, query, header or a hidden field.
- **Why:** Anything the client sends can be edited. Trusting it lets a visitor become an admin or pay one cent.
- **Good:** `const role = (await db.user(session.userId)).role`; the price is read from the product table.
- **Bad:** `if (req.body.role === 'admin')`; `charge(req.body.price)`.
- **Decided by:** read the handlers: role, user id and price come from the session or the database

## Header baseline (gate K)

Three response headers every delivered page sends. They cost nothing and close whole classes of attack.

| ID | Header | Accepted as | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| sec-h1 | `Content-Security-Policy` | the response header, or `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` | limits where scripts, styles and frames can load from |
| sec-h2 | `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` | the response header only (there is no meta equivalent) | stops a browser running a file as a type it wasn't served as |
| sec-h3 | `Referrer-Policy` | the response header, or `<meta name="referrer">` | keeps full URLs (tokens, search terms) out of other sites' logs |

A static page sets headers through its host. For local files the gate reads a `_headers` file next to the page (the
format Netlify and Cloudflare Pages use: a path line, then indented `Name: value` lines), so a template proves its
headers the same way it would ship them.

GitHub Pages cannot set response headers. The templates there carry the CSP and referrer policy as `<meta>` tags (both
work from a meta tag), but `nosniff` has no meta equivalent: gating a live GitHub Pages URL reports sec-h2 until the
site moves to a host that reads `_headers` (or sets headers another way). Say so in the hand-off; don't hide it.
The gate's own browser contexts bypass the page's CSP so its measuring scripts can run; axis K reads the policy, it
doesn't depend on it being enforced.

## Secret patterns gate K looks for

Private key blocks (`-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----`), AWS access key IDs, Stripe live secret keys, GitHub tokens,
Slack tokens, Google API keys, OpenAI/Anthropic-style keys, and `secret`/`token`/`password`/`api_key` assignments to a
long literal. A match is reported with the first and last four characters only, never the whole value. Publishable
keys (`pk_live_…`) are allowed: they are meant for the client.

## Heuristic warnings (gate K, never counted)

| ID | Warns when |
|---|---|
| sec-06 | a script writes a token-, jwt-, auth- or session-named key to `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` |
| sec-08 | a local target's project does not ignore `.env` (`git check-ignore`, else the nearest `.gitignore`); URLs are not checked |
| sec-09 | a `console.*` call prints a token, key, password, session id or email |
| sec-h1 | a URL target (a real server) sends its CSP only as a `<meta>` tag (send it as a header); local files are served by the gate, so they are not judged on this |
| sec-17 | a development build or a source map is on the page |

Each failing gate K item adds one to the K count in the stamp (`rules: C0 S0 P0 K0`); the page passes K at 0.
Warnings are printed under the K line and are not in the count, the stamp or its id.

--- craft/accessibility.md ---
# Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA minimum)

Award-level means usable by everyone. These rules are part of the design, not a final pass.

## Structure
- One `<h1>` per page; headings in order; landmarks (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `footer`).
- Interactive elements are real `<button>` and `<a href>`, never clickable `<div>`s.
- Split text (letters/words) keeps an accessible name: `aria-label` on the parent, `aria-hidden="true"` on the pieces. GSAP SplitText handles this automatically.
- Skip link to `#main` as the first focusable element.

## Visual
- Contrast: 4.5:1 body text, 3:1 large text and UI component boundaries. Verify with `node scripts/contrast.mjs`.

### Contrast rules you can check by reading the CSS

You don't need a browser or a script for these. Read the stylesheet and check each rule. A "no" on any of them is a failure.

1. **Every text token against every surface it sits on.** Write the pairs as a comment next to the tokens, one per surface, with the ratio: `/* --muted on --ground 5.01 · on --surface 4.45 ✗ */`. A tinted panel, card, band or overlay counts as its own surface. Checking only against the page ground isn't enough.
2. **A muted token belongs to one surface.** `--muted` is for `--ground` only. Text inside any rule whose background is something else (`--surface`, `--panel`, a dark band) uses `--ink` or a token made for that surface (`--muted-on-surface`). Read each `color: var(--muted)` and look up the background of that selector and its parents. If it isn't the ground, that's a failure.
3. **Margin for small, muted text.** Muted text below 14px, or mono/uppercase captions, needs at least **4.7:1**, not 4.5. Thin strokes at 11–13px lose contrast to antialiasing, and a 4.45 that rounds to "about 4.5" is a fail.
4. **Small text has a floor.** Nothing below 12px (`0.75rem`). Text under 14px over an image or video sits on a solid chip, never straight on the picture.
5. **No dimming by opacity.** Never lower text contrast with `opacity` or an alpha colour (`rgb(… / .6)`, `#0008`). The ratio then depends on whatever is behind it. Use a solid token with a known ratio.
6. **Text over images or gradients** sits on a solid or scrimmed area with a stated worst-case ratio. Without one, it fails.
7. **No text below 4.5:1 on the ground.** That includes eyebrows, links and accent-coloured labels: an accent that reads as "bold" on screen is often 3.5–4.4:1 at 12px.

Example. In the v2.1.0 studio output, `.plan{background:var(--surface)}` and `.plan figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.7rem}` fail rules 1–3 by reading alone. `--muted` (#6B6157) passes on the ground (#EFE9E1) but sits on `--surface` (#E4DCD1) at 4.45:1, in 11px mono caps. The measured gate (`scripts/gate.mjs`) later confirms the same five captions at 4.45:1.
- Focus is always visible and designed (`:focus-visible` ring in the accent colour, 2px or more, with offset).
- Never convey meaning by colour alone.
- Text resizes to 200% without loss; layout holds at 320px wide.
- Target size: 24×24px minimum (WCAG 2.2), with 44×44px recommended for primary touch targets.

## Motion
- Every animation has a `prefers-reduced-motion` path (see `motion/tokens.md`).
- Nothing flashes more than 3 times per second.
- Anything that moves for longer than 5 seconds (marquees, loops, video) can be paused.
- Scroll is never hijacked: native keyboard scrolling (Space, PageDown, arrows) and screen readers move through content normally. Pinned sections still read in DOM order.

## Interaction
- All functionality works with the keyboard; the focus order follows the visual order.
- Cursor-only effects have a non-cursor equivalent (content visible by default, or tap/Enter to reveal).
- Custom cursors never hide the real pointer for users who need it; respect `(pointer: coarse)`.
- Forms: visible labels, errors in text near the field, `aria-describedby` for hints, `autocomplete` attributes.

## Media
- Meaningful images have `alt` text describing their role; decorative images have `alt=""`.
- Video has captions; autoplay video is muted, has controls and pauses for reduced motion.
- Canvas/WebGL content has a DOM text equivalent.

--- craft/media.md ---
# Media

A visually rich site needs real visual material, not generic gradients, empty image boxes and arbitrary placeholders. A reachable URL is not a licence, though, and a direct image link is not a promise that the file stays online or free for commercial use. This file covers how to find media, track where it came from, and make clear what must be replaced before launch. Referenced from `thinking/sequence.md` ("Required output: by delivery mode"); checked by `scripts/completeness.mjs` (gate axis X).

## Media selection

Every build weighs its need for photography, video, icons, illustrations, SVGs, screenshots and any other visual asset the brief calls for.

- Select media for the actual project: the page content, the visual concept and the audience. Do not force every format into every build.
- Prefer real, relevant assets when they improve the design.
- Use custom illustration, CSS, SVG or another technique when it fits the concept better than stock.

## Where to source media

Starting points:

| Source | For |
|---|---|
| Unsplash — https://unsplash.com | photography |
| Pexels — https://pexels.com | photography, video |
| Pixabay — https://pixabay.com | photography, video, illustration |
| Coverr — https://coverr.co | background video |
| Mixkit — https://mixkit.co | video |
| unDraw — https://undraw.co | illustration |
| Heroicons — https://heroicons.com | icons |
| Lucide — https://lucide.dev | icons |
| Feather — https://feathericons.com | icons |

Other legitimate sources fit the asset type too (the client's own library, a museum's open-access collection, a font or icon set's official repository), on the same terms. These are **discovery sources**, not blanket guarantees. None of them guarantees, for every asset, that it is licence-eligible for this project, free of restrictions, at a stable URL, or available without limits.

- **Verify each selected asset's licence and usage terms** when possible, on the asset's own page and the source's current licence page. Respect attribution, commercial-use restrictions, redistribution rules, hotlinking restrictions and any other condition.
- **Prefer stable integration**: download the file into the project, or use the source's supported method, when the terms permit it. Do not assume an image URL stays available.
- **No keys or paid services by default.** Do not require an API key or a paid service when a suitable free, accessible alternative exists. If a source needs registration, an API key, or has access limits, say so; never claim unrestricted access.

## Placeholder identification

Separate temporary reference assets from assets approved for production.

For a temporary or unverified asset, name it, where practical:

```
placeholder-<description>.<ext>
```

Where appropriate, add a source and replacement comment:

```html
<!-- PLACEHOLDER: <source URL>; license/status: <verified or unknown>; replace before production if not approved. -->
```

```js
// PLACEHOLDER: <source URL>; license/status: <verified or unknown>; replace before production if not approved.
```

When a file cannot reasonably be renamed (a CMS path, a framework-imposed name), record its placeholder status in `MEDIA.md` instead.

Do not label a properly licensed, production-approved asset as disposable just because it came from a stock site.

## Placeholders by delivery mode

The delivery mode (`templates/TEMPLATE.md`) decides how far temporary media may go; for media, "no placeholders" (`SKILL.md`, law 5) means no undocumented or disguised placeholder.

| Mode | Temporary assets |
|---|---|
| Design exploration | allowed when needed, each identified as a placeholder in `MEDIA.md` with its replacement need; never shown as approved |
| Functional frontend | as above; the handoff names what must be replaced before launch |
| Production template | production-bound assets have suitable rights or documented approval; anything still unresolved (rights, attribution, replacement) stays visible in `MEDIA.md` and `README.md`, and the build is not called production-ready |

## Required `MEDIA.md`

Every generated website project has a `MEDIA.md` in its root, in every delivery mode. At least these columns (add `Used where`, `Creator / provider` or `Notes` when they help; skip metadata that doesn't apply):

```markdown
| File or asset | Source URL | License / usage status | Placeholder? | Replace before production? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| public/img/placeholder-harbour-dawn.jpg | <asset page URL> | unknown | yes | yes |
| public/icons/arrow.svg | https://lucide.dev | <licence as stated on the source, verified YYYY-MM-DD> | no | no |
| hero grain (CSS) | custom, this build | custom | no | no |
```

- List the photographs, videos, illustrations, externally sourced icons and other assets the project uses.
- Record the original source where available, the licence or terms that apply, and any attribution or replacement requirement.
- Write `unknown` when the licence or provenance has not been verified. Never invent a licence, attribution, source URL or permission.
- Mark custom-created assets as custom, and list external dependencies (an icon package, a hosted font, a video CDN) where relevant.
- A build with no external media still ships `MEDIA.md`: a short inventory stating that no external media assets were used.

The root `README.md` explains the media handoff and names every unverified or temporary asset that must be reviewed before production.

**What the gate checks** (`scripts/completeness.mjs`, axis X, whenever `DESIGN.md` has a `Scope` section, i.e. website creation): `MEDIA.md` exists and cannot be excused; it has the inventory table, or states that no external media assets were used; each row has a source, a licence / usage status (`unknown` is allowed) and yes / no in both last columns; a placeholder is marked for replacement; an `unknown` or `unverified` licence is marked for replacement; a `placeholder-…` file or a `PLACEHOLDER:` comment in the project is listed; and `README.md` points to `MEDIA.md` when anything still needs replacing. Pending replacements print a not-production-ready warning. It never decides whether a licence is valid. A `Scope` with no `**Delivery mode:**` line (a build from before delivery modes) only warns on a missing `MEDIA.md`, so a fix there doesn't fail. Every local media file (images, video, SVG files; build output and `favicon.*` aside) needs a row: its path, its file name, or a folder row ending in `/`.

**Maintenance** (the light route; no `Scope` section): the gate still checks media, through `maintenanceMedia()` (`node scripts/completeness.mjs <project> --maintenance` without a browser). A fix that adds, replaces, renames or removes media updates the project's `MEDIA.md` in the same change; one that doesn't touch media writes nothing.

| The project has | The check |
|---|---|
| a `MEDIA.md` | the full media check above: an added or renamed file without a row fails, and so does an unlisted placeholder |
| no `MEDIA.md`, no media files | nothing to check |
| no `MEDIA.md`, media files | undocumented `placeholder-…` files and `PLACEHOLDER:` comments fail; with a git baseline, media added or replaced since the last commit fails until `MEDIA.md` exists; without one, a warning that says the check cannot tell what changed |

**Baseline.** In a git working tree with a commit, the last commit is the baseline (`git status`: untracked, added, renamed, modified media). Without git there is none, and the check says so. A file replaced under the same name is detected only with the baseline: if `MEDIA.md` did not change in the same working tree, it warns to confirm the row (a re-export from the same source needs no edit). A media file deleted since the last commit while `MEDIA.md` did not change warns to remove or update its row. Changes already committed are invisible to it, so run the gate before committing the fix. Without a baseline, update the row by hand.

## Media rules

- Do not take assets from Google Images results, Pinterest, social media or arbitrary websites as though finding them there grants reuse rights. Such places may be looked at for design research; implementation assets come through legitimate, documented channels.
- For people, prefer appropriately licensed stock photography, or media the client supplied or explicitly authorized.
- Do not use identifiable real people or public figures as disposable placeholders without appropriate permission and usage rights.
- Do not copy proprietary code, protected artwork or photography from reference websites. Learn composition, art direction, information architecture and interaction from them; build original or properly licensed implementations.
- Every temporary or unverified asset is identifiable (name, comment, or a `MEDIA.md` row) and documented.
- Every website build includes `MEDIA.md`.
- Do not call the site production-ready while required permissions, attributions or placeholder replacements are unresolved.

## Production handoff

Tell the client which assets need replacement, permission checks, attribution or approval before launch (in the handoff message and in `README.md`, pointing to `MEDIA.md`).

A production-approved asset stays in place when its usage rights, provenance, technical suitability and required conditions have been verified.

## What not to do

- Treat a search result or an accessible URL as proof of a licence.
- Ship unverified temporary assets silently.
- Invent provenance or licensing information.
- Omit `MEDIA.md`.
- Make every asset a placeholder by default when it is already approved for production.

--- motion/selection.md ---
# Motion selection: the experience decides the technique

Motion gets the same research and selection as layout. No tool is the universal answer: not GSAP, not Motion (Framer Motion), not CSS, not Lenis, not WebGL. Choose by the experience being designed, the existing stack, and real performance and accessibility constraints. Then apply `motion/tokens.md` (the four questions) and the tier files for the how.

## 1. Classify every significant animation

| Class | Purpose | Rules |
|---|---|---|
| **Functional feedback** | press, hover, focus, selection, validation, toggles, add-to-cart confirmation | always present where it helps; fast (≤ 250ms), never blocks input, works with keyboard and touch |
| **Navigation and state transitions** | page-to-page continuity, opening a sheet, filtering a grid, expanding a row | communicates where something came from or went; interruptible; never delays the task |
| **Decorative** | ambient texture, idle loops, cursor flourishes | earns its place through the Restraint Gate; pausable if it loops; off under reduced motion |
| **Narrative and immersive** | scroll-told chapters, scrubbed sequences, 3D scenes | lives on storytelling surfaces, not task flows; has a designed still for reduced motion |

The experience must stay understandable with every decorative and narrative animation removed. Award-level motion does not mean animating everything; it means the right few things are exceptional.

## 2. Research the motion language

For the chosen direction, ask what motion should communicate, then look at how strong references do it (`craft/research.md`; `references/sources/motion.md`, `references/effects-vocabulary.md`, Codrops, case studies, official docs). Topics worth researching when the project has them:

- typography and text reveals · image and media transitions · scroll-linked storytelling · page transitions
- navigation and menu interactions · product discovery and filtering · hover, focus, selection and feedback states
- cursor interactions (desktop only, never essential) · SVG and vector animation · 3D and shaders (when the concept is spatial, material or lit)
- loading, skeleton and state transitions · microinteractions

Write the motion language in `MOTION.md` as a small set of named behaviours with timing and purpose, consistent across the site, rather than a different preset per section.

## 3. Choose the technique

Pick the lowest-cost technique that delivers the experience, inside the project's stack:

| Need | Usually enough | Reach further when |
|---|---|---|
| Feedback, hover, focus, simple reveals | CSS transitions and `@keyframes`, WAAPI | — |
| Same-page state changes, list reorder, shared elements | View Transitions API, FLIP (CSS or a small helper), Motion layout animations in React | the browser support matrix rules View Transitions out |
| Cross-page continuity | cross-document View Transitions (MPA), framework router transitions | a Tier 3 concept needs choreographed handoffs |
| Scroll-linked effects | CSS scroll-driven animations (`animation-timeline`) with a fallback | sequencing, pinning or scrubbing needs GSAP ScrollTrigger |
| Text splitting and kinetic type | CSS on hand-split spans | GSAP SplitText for line-aware splits |
| React component motion | Motion (Framer Motion) when the project already uses React | heavy timelines suit GSAP better |
| Illustrated or vector motion | SVG + CSS, Lottie or Rive for designer-made assets | — |
| Smooth scroll | native scroll | a scrubbed timeline genuinely needs sync; Lenis is optional (`principles/heuristics.md` → H-lenis) |
| Spatial, material, lit concepts | CSS 3D transforms | Three.js / OGL / shaders, with a static fallback and a performance check |

A library the project doesn't have is a dependency: it passes `craft/components.md`. Don't add GSAP for three fades, or WebGL for a gradient.

## 4. Guardrails

- **Respect reduced motion** with a designed still, not "animations off" (`principles/restraint.md` → Designed stills).
- **No scroll hijacking.** Native scroll speed, direction and keyboard paging keep working; pinned sections are short and escapable.
- **Restrained parallax:** small offsets, never on text the user is reading.
- **No perpetual distraction** near tasks; loops pause off-screen and on request.
- **Never block a task** with an animation: checkout, search, filtering and forms respond immediately.
- **Touch and keyboard paths** exist for every hover or cursor behaviour that carries meaning.

## 5. Validate in a real browser

Animation code is not evidence. Run the page: the gate's motion checks (`scripts/gate.mjs`), reduced motion emulated, a 4× CPU throttle on the heaviest chapter, a phone-sized viewport with touch, and keyboard-only navigation through each core journey. Record what was checked in `GATE.md`.

--- motion/tokens.md ---
# Motion Tokens and the Four Questions

Load this before any motion work.

## The four questions (every animation must pass)

1. **Should it move at all?** How often will people see it? Something seen 100 times a day (a menu, a button) gets the smallest, fastest motion or none. Something seen once (the hook, a story chapter) can be rich.
2. **What does it tell the user?** Pick one: *where something came from · where it went · what changed · what to look at · what this brand feels like*. If none applies, delete it.
3. **What's the cheapest tool that does it?** CSS before GSAP, GSAP before WebGL, and the cheapest property: see "What to animate" below.
4. **What happens at the edges?** Interruption (hover out mid-animation), exit, rapid repeat, reduced motion, touch, slow devices.

## What to animate (the one rule)

Every tier file, `principles/restraint.md` and `craft/performance.md` point here. Costs and the checks behind them are in `craft/performance.md` ("What each property costs", P1–P2).

| Property | Rule |
|---|---|
| `transform`, `opacity` | **Prefer.** The default for all routine motion: reveals, parallax, hovers, pins, page transitions |
| `clip-path` (basic shapes: `inset()`, `circle()`, `polygon()`) | **Allowed when it serves the concept**: the shape opening *is* the idea (an iris into the next chapter, an image unmasking inside a pinned chapter, a wipe between scenes). Not as decoration on routine reveals. The reduced-motion state shows the content unclipped |
| `filter` (blur, drop-shadow), `backdrop-filter`, `mask`, `box-shadow`, moving gradients, large-area `clip-path` | **Paint-heavy: only with a performance check and a static fallback.** Limit it to one element (or write down why it covers more), measure the frame time under a 4× CPU throttle at the largest viewport it appears at, and ship a static version that keeps the meaning when it is off |
| `width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`, `padding`, `font-size`, grid tracks | **Never animated.** Use `transform` (scale, translate) or FLIP |

The library follows it: `examples/components/scroll/aperture.tsx` and `examples/scroll/pinned-chapter.tsx` animate `clip-path` because the opening is the concept, and both show the content fully open under reduced motion.

## Easing tokens

```css
:root {
  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);       /* UI default: fast start, gentle stop */
  --ease-out-strong: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1); /* editorial reveals, house signature */
  --ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);    /* things moving on screen from A to B */
  --ease-drawer: cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1);    /* sheets, drawers, panels */
  --ease-linear: linear;                            /* scrubbed scroll, progress, marquees */
}
```

GSAP equivalents: `"power3.out"` / `"expo.out"` for reveals, `"power2.inOut"` for travel, `"none"` inside scrubbed timelines.

**Never** use ease-in for things entering. It feels sluggish exactly when the user is waiting.

## Duration tokens

| Use | Duration |
|---|---|
| Press / tap feedback | 80–140ms |
| Hover, focus, toggles | 120–200ms |
| Tooltips, small popovers | 140–220ms |
| Dropdowns, menus | 180–260ms |
| Modals, drawers | 240–420ms |
| Long-form text reveals | 600–1000ms (+ stagger 60–120ms) |
| Page transitions | 400–700ms |
| Scrubbed scroll | no duration: tied to scroll distance |

Exit animations run about 20–30% faster than entrances.

## Distance and scale

- Entrances travel 8–40px (UI) or 100% of a masked line (editorial). Never fly in from off-screen without a reason.
- Scale entrances start at 0.94–0.98, **never `scale(0)`**.
- Press states scale to 0.96–0.98.

## Reduced motion (required everywhere)

Reduced motion doesn't mean nothing at all. It means **no travel, no parallax, no scrubbing, no autoplay**:
- Replace movement with an instant change or a fade of 150ms or less.
- Pinned Motomation chapters become static sequential frames (see Tier 5).
- Smooth scroll is off (Lenis does this by default with `respectReducedMotion`).

GSAP pattern, used in every example:

```js
const mm = gsap.matchMedia();
mm.add(
  { motion: "(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)", reduce: "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)",
    desktop: "(min-width: 900px)", touch: "(hover: none)" },
  (ctx) => {
    const { motion, desktop, touch } = ctx.conditions;
    if (!motion) return;          // final states already set in CSS
    // build animations here; they are reverted automatically when conditions change
  }
);
```

## Pointer and touch

- Hover effects only when `(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`.
- Cursor-follow effects are off on touch. Provide the content another way (tap to open, visible by default).
- Magnetic or tilt effects never move the hit area away from the finger or pointer.

--- motion/tier-1-basic.md ---
# Tier 1: Basic (CSS)

Every site has Tier 1. It's the craft floor.

## What belongs here

- Hover, focus-visible, active and disabled states on every interactive element.
- Underline draws, colour shifts, small lifts (`translateY(-2px)`), press scale (0.97).
- CSS `@keyframes` for loaders and small loops.
- Same-document View Transitions for state changes (filters, tabs, theme switch).
- CSS scroll-driven animations (`animation-timeline: view()`) as progressive enhancement.

## Recipes

```css
/* link underline that draws from the left and retracts to the right */
.link { background: linear-gradient(currentColor 0 0) 0 100% / 0 1px no-repeat;
        transition: background-size 220ms var(--ease-out); }
.link:hover { background-size: 100% 1px; }

/* button press */
.btn { transition: transform 120ms var(--ease-out), background-color 160ms var(--ease-out); }
.btn:active { transform: scale(0.97); }

/* focus that is designed, not removed */
:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 2px; }

/* reveal on entering view: progressive enhancement */
@supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .reveal { animation: reveal linear both; animation-timeline: view(); animation-range: entry 10% cover 30%; }
    @keyframes reveal { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(24px); } }
  }
}

/* theme switch with a view transition */
::view-transition-old(root), ::view-transition-new(root) { animation-duration: 420ms; }
```

```js
function setTheme(next) {
  const apply = () => document.documentElement.dataset.theme = next;
  if (!document.startViewTransition || matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) return apply();
  document.startViewTransition(apply);
}
```

## Rules

- What you may animate follows the one rule in `motion/tokens.md` ("What to animate"): `transform` and `opacity` by default; `clip-path` when the shape change is the concept; paint-heavy properties only with a perf check and a static fallback; layout properties never.
- Transition only what changes: `transition: transform 160ms, opacity 160ms`, never `transition: all`.
- Use `@media (hover: hover)` to scope hover effects.
- Use the `motion/tokens.md` durations. UI feedback (press, hover, focus, toggles, tooltips) stays at or under <!--rule:ui_feedback_max_ms-->250ms<!--/rule-->; view transitions use the page-transition range in `motion/tokens.md`.

